A login handler builds its query like this. Which change actually prevents SQL injection?

```js
const sql = "SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = '" + email + "'";
await db.query(sql);
```

In PostgreSQL 18, the `reporting` role was granted access like this. Which query by `reporting` fails?

```sql
CREATE TABLE emp (id int PRIMARY KEY, name text, salary numeric);
GRANT SELECT (id, name) ON emp TO reporting;
```

A new role can read a table, yet this fails. Why?

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA hr;
CREATE TABLE hr.pay (id int, amount numeric);
GRANT SELECT ON hr.pay TO rep;

SET ROLE rep;
SELECT * FROM hr.pay;
-- ERROR: permission denied for schema hr
```

A multi-tenant table uses this policy. A new code path forgets to set `app.tenant` before querying. What does the `app` role see?

```sql
ALTER TABLE docs ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON docs
  USING (tenant = current_setting('app.tenant', true));
```

With the session set to tenant `a`, the `app` role runs an INSERT. What happens?

```sql
CREATE POLICY p ON docs
  USING (tenant = current_setting('app.tenant', true));
-- no WITH CHECK clause; app has INSERT on docs

SET app.tenant = 'a';
INSERT INTO docs (id, tenant, body) VALUES (3, 'b', 'z');
```

To stop users promoting themselves to admin through a profile API, the DBA grants this. Which statements run by `webapp` succeed? (Choose two.)

```sql
CREATE TABLE prof (id int, email text, role text);
GRANT SELECT, UPDATE (email) ON prof TO webapp;
```

`lead` received SELECT with grant option and passed it on to `junior`. What happens when the DBA runs this?

```sql
GRANT SELECT ON proj TO lead WITH GRANT OPTION;
-- as lead:
GRANT SELECT ON proj TO junior;
-- as DBA:
REVOKE SELECT ON proj FROM lead;
```

A team exposes only names from a sensitive table through a view. `viewer` has no privileges on `salaries`. Which statements are true in PostgreSQL 18? (Choose two.)

```sql
CREATE VIEW pub_names AS SELECT name FROM salaries;
GRANT SELECT ON pub_names TO viewer;

CREATE VIEW pub_names_inv WITH (security_invoker = true)
  AS SELECT name FROM salaries;
GRANT SELECT ON pub_names_inv TO viewer;
```

A migration runs in one transaction. Why does the second statement fail in PostgreSQL 18, and what is the fix?

```sql
BEGIN;
ALTER TYPE status ADD VALUE 'refunded';
UPDATE orders SET status = 'refunded' WHERE refund_id IS NOT NULL;
COMMIT;
```

`pp` has rows 1 `(a=true, b=false)`, 2 `(a=false, b=true)` and 3 `(a=false, b=false)`. RLS is enabled, and `app` has SELECT. Which rows does `app` see?

```sql
CREATE POLICY p1 ON pp USING (a);
CREATE POLICY p2 ON pp USING (b);
CREATE POLICY p3 ON pp AS RESTRICTIVE USING (a);
```
